
The simulation runs in the context of an NHS clinical microbiology laboratory

Staffing:
There are three groups of staff:
    Healthcare science support workers (HSSW): These fulfill the role of technicians in receiving and booking in samples,
    plating, and setting up susceptibility testing
    Biomedical Scientists (BMS): Read initial plate results, read susceptibilities and input results onto the laboratory information system
    Clinical Microbiologists: Doctors who verify the laboratory results on the computer system

Samples:
    Samples arrive in the laboratory from the community and the hospital.
    There are a number of specimen types with different inter-arrival means. These will eventually be inferred from testing data
    Different sample types have a different probability distribution of being positive, with different organisms
    Each sample will come from a patient. For now each sample will come from a unique patient
    Blood culture, fluid and tissue samples arrive as individual samples. urine, swabs, stool, swabs, muti-site screens and sputum arrive as batches

Capacity:
    Blood culture bottles incubate separately from other samples; the lab can incubate up to 2,200 blood culture bottles at once
    Every other sample is plated by the HSSW; the lab can store up to 5,000 plates in incubation at once
    For now, model each sample as generating one plate. This will need to change, as samples can generate multiple plates

 Patients:
    Samples are generated from patients
    Patient have an age and gender characteristic will influence sample positivity rate and the organism identified
    In time further metadata will influence sample characteristics

Workflow:
    Samples arrive in the laboratory reception as above and are booked into the system when HCCW are availably.
    Blood cultures
        Are loaded onto the incubator and left for up to 5 days.
        If either blood culture bottle flags it will have a gram stain performed by the BMS, followed by incubation on a primary plate, along with direct susceptibility testing
        The identification is performed 4hrs into incubation - the probability of a successful idenfication is dependent on the organism but can be modelled later
        susceptibility testing is repeated from the primary plate. further susceptibility testing may be required for resistant organisms but this out of context for this model version
    Urine cultures
        Once received they loaded onto an automated cell counter. Samples with a WCC count of > 100 are cultured. Samples with <WCC are rejected unless the patient is pregnant, <12 years of age or from certain clinical areas.
        Loading onto the cell counter, and inocculation of primary plates is performed by an HCCW
        The following day the BMS will read the plates and any positives will have susceptibility testing
        The subsequent day the BMS will read the susceptibilities.
        CUH data suggests ~ 63.9 (SD 1.9%) of samples meet culture criteria.
        Rejected samples are discarded after cell counting
